// the STILL-OPEN half: a real file, a member that does not exist, nothing cites the alias.
import group_lagrange <<< "std/group/lagrange.b4m"

sort G = group_lagrange.Grp
fact lagrange = group_lagrange.noSuchTheoremAtAll

// `2b4m check` on this file prints "OK: 3 declarations, 0 theorems proven", exit 0.
// `noSuchTheoremAtAll` is not in std/group/lagrange.b4m. Nothing cites `lagrange`, and an
// uncited `fact` alias is never resolved (the engine is demand-driven), so the dangling
// member is never looked up.
//
// The IMPORT half of this bug was closed 2026-10-20 (parser.zig `unused_imports` fails on
// an import nothing cites) — note the `sort` above is what makes the import used here, so
// that check correctly stays quiet. What remains is a dangling MEMBER of a real file.
// Candidate fix: resolve every `fact` alias AT DECLARATION rather than on first citation.

// CORRECTION 2026-10-20: the framing above ("resolve fact aliases at declaration") is not
// the right fix. The defect class is an UNUSED DECLARATION, not a dangling target -- the bad
// target is merely what makes it visible. And it cannot be a per-file check: measured, there
// are 470 distinct downstream references to non-fact forwards (835 total), e.g.
// std/permutation/simplicity.b4m uses permutation.transposition 98 times where that name is
// an alias in std/permutation.b4m. A facade re-exporting its sub-theories' vocabulary is the
// intended practice, so "unused in its own file" does not mean dead.
// It belongs in `--library`, blocked on there being no symbol-usage graph (axiom_taint and
// friends are all fact-level). See AATA-fixes.md "1. The engine question".
